{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>אַף חֹבֵב עַמִּים</b> (Yea, He who embraces the peoples). They explained it as being from the Aramaic language, as in ותהון קדמי חביבין (and they were beloved before Me) (Exodus 19:5). He would say: even He who loves Israel, who are many peoples, in the manner of אַחֲרֶיךָ בִנְיָמִין בַּעֲמָמֶיךָ (after you, Benjamin, among your peoples) (Judges 5:14). And וְכָל קְדֹשָׁיו — all His holy ones of Israel, these are the Levites; they are בְּיָדֶךָ (in Your hand), for You have brought them near to Yourself to camp around the Ark. But what appears correct to me is that <b>חֹבֵב</b> derives from the stem of לִטְמוֹן in בְּחֻבִּי עֲוֹנִי (Job 31:33), from the notion of חֶדֶר בְּחֶדֶר לְהֵחָבֵא (2 Chronicles 18:24), and there שָׁם חֶבְיוֹן עֻזּוֹ (Habakkuk 3:4). He would say: even You, in Your hands, hide and conceal the peoples — all the holy ones of Israel mentioned above. He hints that the entire congregation, all of them, are holy, and HaShem conceals them in His hand, in the sense of וָאָשִׂם דְּבָרַי בְּפִיךָ וּבְצֵל יָדִי כִּסִּיתִיךָ (Isaiah 51:16), in the manner of סִתְרִי וּמָגִנִּי אַתָּה (Psalms 119:114), and likewise וְיִתְיָעֲצוּ עַל צְפוּנֶיךָ (Psalms 83:4). And this is a hint to what is said (Numbers 14:14): אֲשֶׁר עַיִן בְּעַיִן נִרְאָה אַתָּה ה' וַעֲנָנְךָ עֹמֵד עֲלֵיהֶם, and so He said in the Song (Deuteronomy 32:10): יְסֹבְבֶנְהוּ יְבוֹנְנֵהוּ יִצְּרֶנְהוּ כְּאִישׁוֹן עֵינוֹ." "<b>וְהֵם תֻּכּוּ</b> (and they were struck). Like הֻכּוּ (they were struck), as in כַּאֲשֶׁר בָּא תִּרְגַּלְתִּי לְאֶפְרַיִם (Hosea 11:3) in place of הִרְגַּלְתִּי, and as when the feminine הֵ\"א is replaced by תָּי\"ו in אִם אֶתֵּן שְׁנַת לְעֵינָי (Psalms 132:4), עֹשֶׂה רָע מְאַת (Ecclesiastes 8:12), and likewise many others. He says that they were struck with every plague in the wilderness to go after You wherever You go — they paid no heed to hunger or thirst or the plague of serpent and scorpion, but went out at Your feet and ran after You. And this is in the sense of what is said (Jeremiah 2:2): זָכַרְתִּי לָךְ חֶסֶד נְעוּרַיִךְ אַהֲבַת כְּלוּלֹתָיִךְ לֶכְתֵּךְ אַחֲרַי בַּמִּדְבָּר בְּאֶרֶץ לֹא זְרוּעָה." "<b>יִשָּׂא מִדַּבְּרֹתֶיךָ</b> (He shall lift up Your utterances). Like לֹא תִשָּׂא אֶת שֵׁם ה' אֱלֹהֶיךָ לַשָּׁוְא (Exodus 20:7), וּבַל אֶשָּׂא אֶת שְׁמוֹתָם עַל שְׂפָתָי (Psalms 16:4), יִשָּׂא בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא לֵאמֹר (Isaiah 50:7). מִדַּבְּרֹתֶיךָ — like וּמִדְבַּרֵךְ נָאוֶה (Song of Songs 4:3). He says that he shall bear Your words upon his lips and mutter them constantly."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "Ramban disputes the standard interpretation by deriving חֹבֵב from a root meaning 'to hide/conceal' rather than 'to love'; he marshals parallel forms from Job, Chronicles, and Habakkuk.", 1 "Ramban explains the unusual form תֻּכּוּ as a passive with תָּי\"ו replacing the expected feminine/collective הֵ\"א, citing analogous forms in Psalms and Ecclesiastes.", 2 "Ramban reads מִדַּבְּרֹתֶיךָ as 'Your utterances/speech' (from the root דבר in the hiphil/piel), supporting this with Song of Songs 4:3; the verb יִשָּׂא is taken in the sense of bearing upon the lips."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:17.287102951Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}